<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vlad the Impaler</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1430–<a href="/years/1477">1477</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Vlad III Dracula, Vlad Țepeș</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Vlad III, known as Vlad the Impaler or Dracula, was voivode of Wallachia in 1448, from 1456 to 1462 and in 1476. Notorious for impaling enemies, he fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> and in 1462 launched a night attack on the camp of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mehmed-ii">Mehmed II</a>, but was soon driven out and held prisoner in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>. He was killed in 1476 or 1477. His name later inspired Bram Stoker's Dracula.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vlad-the-impaler.jpg" alt="Vlad the Impaler" width="320" height="403" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vlad_%C5%A2epe%C5%9F,_the_Impaler,_Prince_of_Wallachia_(1456-1462)_(died_1477).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Vlad was the second legitimate son of Vlad II Dracul, who took his nickname from membership of the Order of the Dragon, a fraternity founded by Sigismund of Luxemburg, King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, to halt the Ottoman advance. Dracula is the Slavonic genitive, meaning the son of Dracul. In modern Romanian dracul means the devil, which added to his reputation.</p><p>The historian Radu Florescu writes that Vlad was born in Sighișoara, where his father lived from 1431 to 1435. In 1442 Vlad and his younger brother Radu were held as hostages in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> to secure their father's loyalty. In 1447 his father and eldest brother Mircea were murdered after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-hunyadi">John Hunyadi</a> invaded Wallachia and installed Vladislav II as voivode.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>Vlad took Wallachia briefly with Ottoman support in October 1448, but Vladislav returned and Vlad fled by 7 December. After exile in Moldavia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> he invaded Wallachia with Hungarian support in 1456, and Vladislav died during the invasion. Vlad purged the boyars and came into conflict with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transylvanian-saxons">Transylvanian Saxons</a>, whose villages he plundered, impaling the captives, which gave him his epithet.</p><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mehmed-ii">Mehmed II</a> demanded personal homage, Vlad had the sultan's two envoys captured and impaled. In January 1462 he attacked Ottoman territory, massacring tens of thousands of Turks and Muslim Bulgarians. He tried to capture the sultan at Târgoviște on the night of 16 to 17 June 1462. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> withdrew, but many Wallachians deserted to Radu.</p><p>In late 1462 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matthias-corvinus">Matthias Corvinus</a>, King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, had Vlad captured. To justify this to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-pius-ii">Pope Pius II</a> and the Venetians he produced letters said to be Vlad's, which most historians regard as forged. Vlad was held at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/visegrad">Visegrád</a> from 1463 to 1475 and released at the request of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stephen-the-great">Stephen III of Moldavia</a>. He was reputedly made to convert to Roman Catholicism. After fighting with Corvinus's army in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia">Bosnia</a>, he regained Wallachia in his last attempt to hold the throne, but Basarab Laiotă returned with Ottoman support. Vlad was killed in battle near Snagov before 10 January 1477.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Stories of his cruelty circulated in his lifetime, and courtiers of Corvinus promoted them after his arrest. The poet Michael Beheim wrote a poem about him that was performed at the court of Emperor Frederick III in 1463, and such books were among the first bestsellers in German-speaking lands. Some in his region regarded him as a folk hero for opposing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>. His name is linked to Bram Stoker's vampire Count Dracula of 1897, although Stoker's notes do not name Vlad directly. Stoker learned of vampires from Emily Gerard's article on Transylvanian superstitions, published in 1885. Tursun Beg called him Kazıklı Voyvoda, the Impaler Lord.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Vlad the Impaler” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Vlad III Dracula</b>, released by the Ottomans to rule in 1448, resisted them fiercely until Mehmed the Conqueror defeated him in 1462 after the Night Attack at Târgoviște, forcing a retreat and a higher tribute.</blockquote><a href="/empire/principality-of-wallachia">Principality of Wallachia · Military</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Sighișoara, in what is now <a href="/countries/romania">Romania</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bucharest">Bucharest</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/romania">Romania</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=46.22,24.79&amp;year=1460" aria-label="Sighișoara on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Vlad the Impaler"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4692 6155h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4692 6155h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=46.22,24.79&amp;year=1460">What was here in 1460? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
